# Env -- real credential files never enter Git; only *.env.example templates do.
# `*.env` catches any prefixed variant (backend.env, prod.env); `**/.env` catches
# a .env in any subdirectory. The negations keep the committed templates visible.
.env
.env.local
# MBS.SC: the scanner execution plane's own credential file. `*.env` does NOT match a
# name of the form `.env.<suffix>` (no character precedes the dot), and neither does
# `.env` -- so this needs its own rule or a real SCANNER_WORKER_TOKEN could be committed.
# `.env.scanner.example` stays visible via the !**/.env.example negation... it does not:
# that negation only matches a file literally NAMED .env.example, so the template is
# re-included explicitly below.
.env.scanner
!.env.scanner.example
*.env
**/.env
**/.env.local
apps/web/.env.local
!.env.example
!**/.env.example

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Node
node_modules/
apps/web/.next/
apps/web/out/
npm-debug.log*
*.tsbuildinfo

# Docker
*.pid

# Local TLS-interception CA + its build overlay -- developer-machine only, never
# committed. The images take this root as an OPTIONAL BuildKit secret; CI and
# production build without it and trust public CAs only. Exact paths (not *.crt /
# *.cer) so legitimate project certificates are never hidden by accident.
infra/docker/norton-webmail-root.crt
infra/docker/norton-webmail-root.cer
infra/docker-compose.local-ca.yml

# Production Docker Secrets. docker-compose.prod.yml mounts every credential from
# infra/secrets/*.txt -- database DSN, Redis/Postgres/MinIO passwords, JWT and API keys.
# These are generated per-deployment on the host and must NEVER be committed. The
# directory itself is ignored so a new secret file cannot be added by accident.
infra/secrets/

# MBS.SC P8-D -- runtime operational flags. `infra/runtime/` is bind-mounted read-only into
# the scanner-manager; an operator creates EMERGENCY_DISABLE_PRIVATE_SCANNING there to stop
# all private scanning platform-wide within ~5s without a restart. It is per-deployment
# operational STATE, never source: committing a sentinel would ship a kill switch stuck ON
# (or mask one an operator had set). The directory is ignored so no flag can be added by
# accident; .gitkeep is tracked so the mount point exists in a fresh checkout.
infra/runtime/*
!infra/runtime/.gitkeep

# Claude Code local session state (agent worktrees, etc.) -- already excluded from
# the Docker build context via .dockerignore.
.claude/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/

# Alembic
db/migrations/*.sqlite3

# Coverage artifacts (generated by the CI/local coverage gate -- Phase 1.7)
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# Ad-hoc audit/report artifacts (not source)
backend_full_review.pdf
MBS_PT_Backend_Production_Audit_Report.pdf

# AUDIT-012: ad-hoc local RUN artifacts -- dev-server logs and captured test output.
#
# These are produced by running the stack by hand (uvicorn/next dev/pytest redirected to a
# file). They are regenerable, machine-specific, and can contain local paths, hostnames and
# stack traces, so they must never be committed. Ignored by exact name rather than a blanket
# `*.log` / `*.txt`: this repo tracks legitimate .txt/.md content, and an over-broad rule is
# exactly how a real file ends up silently invisible to git.
#
# NOTE: the three log files below are currently present in this working tree and are cited as
# EVIDENCE by mbs_security_toolchain_forensic_analysis.md (the captured
# `kombu ... Error 11001 connecting to redis:6379` traceback). They are deliberately left ON
# DISK and merely un-committable -- deleting referenced audit evidence would be worse than
# keeping an ignored file.
uvicorn.log
web.log
celery_test_output.txt
pytest_output.txt
home.html

# Supply-chain scan outputs (generated by CI/local runner -- Phase 1.8)
sbom.cdx.json
pip-audit-report.json
trivy-*-report.json
gitleaks-report.json
